{"p1":[{"id":"p1:2010M_TZ1_35","s":"May 2010 TZ1","k":"2010M_TZ1","n":35,"img":"data:image/png;base64,","a":"B","key":"With limited oxygen, hydrocarbons burn to <b>carbon monoxide</b> (and/or carbon) plus <b>water</b>. Hydrogen and methane are not combustion products; H is always oxidized to H<sub>2</sub>O.","lvl":"SL","src":"ms","flag":"","h":223},{"id":"p1:2013M_TZ2_33","s":"May 2013 TZ2","k":"2013M_TZ2","n":33,"img":"data:image/png;base64,","a":"C","key":"Incomplete combustion of a C/H/O fuel in limited oxygen gives carbon monoxide and/or carbon (soot), plus water. Hydrogen is never a combustion product, and CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O only (D) means complete combustion.","lvl":"SL","src":"ms","flag":"","h":223}]}